.TH AGENTENV 1 "2026-05-24" "agentenv" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
agentenv \- AI agent environment management CLI

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B agentenv
.RI [ global-flags ] "command" " [ command-flags ] [" args "...]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B agentenv
is an environment manager for AI agents \(em think conda, but for skills, MCP
servers, agents, tools, hooks, and prompts.

It provides isolated, reproducible environments under
.IR ~/.agentenv/ .
Environments are defined declaratively in
.IR agent.yaml ,
dependencies are resolved with a PubGrub CDCL solver into
.IR agent.lock ,
and packages are fetched into a content-addressed store with symlink
dedup and garbage collection.

.B agentenv
supports 4 framework adapters (Claude Code, OpenCode, Cursor, Codex), 6
package types (skill, mcp, agent, tool, hook, prompt), and 6 source
schemes (github, npm, local, git, file, url).

.SH OPTIONS
The following options are available for all commands:
.TP
.B \-\-json
Enable JSON output format for machine-readable results.
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Enable debug output with stack traces on errors.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Show help for the given command.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show version information.

.SH COMMANDS
.SS Environment Lifecycle
.TP
.B create
.B create <name>
Create a new agent environment under
.IR ~/.agentenv/envs/<name>/ .
Initializes the environment directory with an
.I agent.yaml
manifest and a
.I state.json
tracking file.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-agent <name>
Agent framework to use (default: claude-code).
.TP
.B \-\-from <env>
Source environment or template to copy from.
.TP
.B \-\-empty
Skip creating agent.yaml (cold-start env).
.RE

.TP
.B delete
.B delete <name>
Delete an environment and all its files from
.IR ~/.agentenv/envs/<name>/ .
The operation is irreversible.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-force
Force deletion even if the environment is active.
.RE

.TP
.B list
List all agent environments under
.IR ~/.agentenv/envs/ .
Displays the name, agent framework, and activation status for each
environment.

.SS Package Management
.TP
.B add
.B add <type> <name> \-\-source <url>
Add a package to the currently active environment's
.IR agent.yaml .
.RS
Supported types: skill, mcp, agent, tool, hook, prompt.
.TP
.B \-\-source <url>
Package source URL (required). Supported schemes:
.RS
github:owner/repo[/path]
.br
npm:@scope/package
.br
local:./path or local:/absolute/path
.br
git:https://example.com/repo.git
.br
file:/absolute/path
.br
url:https://example.com/pkg.tar.gz
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-version <constraint>
Version constraint, e.g. ^1.0.0 or >=2.0.
.TP
.B \-\-dev
Mark as development dependency.
.TP
.B \-\-dry-run
Show what would be added without modifying files.
.RE

.TP
.B remove
.B remove [<type>] <name>
Remove a package from the currently active environment's
.IR agent.yaml .
If
.I <type>
is omitted, all sections are searched. If the name is found in more
than one section, the type must be specified.

.TP
.B list-packages
.B list-packages [name]
List all packages (skills, MCPs, agents, tools, hooks, prompts) defined
in an environment's
.IR agent.yaml .
If name is omitted, the currently active environment is used.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-type <type>
Filter by package type.
.TP
.B \-\-tree
Show a hierarchical view grouped by type.
.RE

.SS Dependency Resolution
.TP
.B lock
.B lock [name]
Read
.IR agent.yaml ,
resolve all dependencies using the PubGrub CDCL solver, and generate
.IR agent.lock .
The environment to lock is determined by: (1) a given name, (2) the
current directory if it has an
.IR agent.yaml ,
or (3) the active environment.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-strict
Fail on non-deterministic or conflicting constraints.
.RE

.TP
.B install
.B install [name]
Read
.IR agent.lock
(or
.I agent.yaml
if no lockfile) and fetch all packages into the local content-addressed
store at
.IR ~/.agentenv/store/ .
If the environment is currently active, trigger re-activation.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-file <path>
Use an alternate agent.yaml file.
.RE

.SS Environment Activation
.TP
.B activate
.B activate <name>
Activate an environment: install all packages (skills, MCP servers,
agents) into the current framework's configuration. Activation is
transactional \(em it backs up your framework config, validates the new
config, applies changes, and rolls back on failure.

This command is typically invoked through the shell function set up by
.BR "agentenv init" .

.TP
.B deactivate
Deactivate the currently active environment: remove all managed skills,
MCP servers, and agents from the framework config, restore the
pre-activation MCP configuration, and remove the ACTIVE lock.

This command is typically invoked through the shell function set up by
.BR "agentenv init" .

.SS Information
.TP
.B info
.B info [name]
Show detailed information about an environment, including name, path,
agent framework, package count, creation date, and activation status.
If name is omitted, the currently active environment is shown.

.TP
.B list-adapters
List all registered agent adapters with their display names.
Available adapters:
.RS
.TP
claude-code
Claude Code
.TP
opencode
OpenCode
.TP
cursor
Cursor
.TP
codex
Codex
.RE

.SS Shell Integration
.TP
.B init
.B init <shell>
Generate a shell init script that integrates
.B agentenv
with your shell. This defines the
.B agentenv()
shell function, enabling environment activation within the current
shell session.
.RS
Supported shells: zsh, bash.
.TP
.B \-\-no-cd-hook
Disable the auto-activation cd hook.
.RE
Usage in your shell config:
.RS
.nf
eval "$(agentenv init zsh)"   # add to ~/.zshrc
eval "$(agentenv init bash)"  # add to ~/.bashrc
.fi
.RE

.TP
.B completion
.B completion [bash|zsh|fish|powershell]
Generate shell completion script for
.B agentenv
commands. The completion script can be sourced to enable tab
completion.
.RS
Examples:
.nf
source <(agentenv completion bash)
source <(agentenv completion zsh)
.fi
To make it permanent:
.nf
agentenv completion bash > /etc/bash_completion.d/agentenv
agentenv completion zsh > "${fpath[1]}/_agentenv"
.fi
.RE

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HOME
Used to locate the
.IR ~/.agentenv/
directory. Must be set and accessible.
.TP
.B VERSION
If set, overrides the embedded version string (used in development).

.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.agentenv/
Root directory for all agentenv data.
.TP
.I ~/.agentenv/envs/<name>/
Environment directory containing
.IR agent.yaml ,
.IR agent.lock ,
and
.IR state.json .
.TP
.I ~/.agentenv/ACTIVE
Lock file indicating the currently active environment. Format:
.I <name>:<framework>
or legacy
.I <name>
format.
.TP
.I ~/.agentenv/store/
Content-addressed package store with symlink dedup and garbage
collection.
.TP
.I ~/.agentenv/backups/
Backup directory containing pre-activation framework config snapshots.
.TP
.I agent.yaml
Environment definition file. Declares the environment name,
description, and all packages with their sources, versions, and
configuration.
.TP
.I agent.lock
Deterministic lockfile generated by
.BR "agentenv lock" .
Contains resolved versions, SHA-256 integrity hashes, and transitive
dependency information.

.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Quick Start
.nf
# Create a workspace with a specific framework
agentenv create my-env --agent opencode

# Add packages from anywhere
agentenv add skill code-reviewer --source github:myorg/reviewer --version "^0.5"
agentenv add mcp filesystem --source npm:@modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem \\
  --command npx --arg -y --arg @modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem --arg /tmp

# Resolve: PubGrub finds the best version for every package
agentenv lock

# Fetch packages into the store
agentenv install

# Activate: inject into your framework
agentenv activate my-env

# Done \(em your agent is ready to use all packages
agentenv deactivate  # switch back when done
.fi

.SS Full Workflow
.nf
# 1. Create a project environment
cd ~/projects/my-ai-project
agentenv create .

# 2. Add packages of different types
agentenv add skill code-reviewer --source github:myorg/code-review-skill
agentenv add mcp filesystem \\
  --source npm:@modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem \\
  --command npx --arg -y \\
  --arg @modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem \\
  --arg /tmp/projects
agentenv add agent my-helper --source github:myorg/helper-agent

# 3. Check the declaration
cat agent.yaml

# 4. Resolve dependencies
agentenv lock

# 5. Inspect the lockfile
cat agent.lock

# 6. Download packages into the store
agentenv install

# 7. Activate (installs into your framework)
agentenv activate my-ai-project

# 8. Verify what is installed
agentenv list-packages
agentenv info my-ai-project

# 9. When done
agentenv deactivate
.fi

.SS Shell Integration with cd Hook
.nf
# Add to ~/.zshrc or ~/.bashrc
eval "$(agentenv init zsh)"

# Now cd into any directory with agent.yaml to auto-activate
cd ~/projects/my-ai-project
# agentenv auto-activates the environment
.fi

.SS JSON Output for Scripting
.nf
# Get environment info as JSON
agentenv info my-env --json | jq .

# List all environments
agentenv list --json | jq '.[].name'

# List packages with versions
agentenv list-packages --json | jq '[.[] | {name, type, resolved}]'
.fi

.SS Managing Environments
.nf
# List all environments
agentenv list

# Show details for an environment
agentenv info my-env

# Delete an environment
agentenv delete my-env

# Force delete an active environment
agentenv delete my-env --force
.fi

.SS Exploring Framework Adapters
.nf
# List available adapters
agentenv list-adapters

# Create an environment for a specific framework
agentenv create --agent opencode my-opencode-env
agentenv create --agent cursor my-cursor-env
agentenv create --agent codex my-codex-env
.fi

.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B agentenv
exits with 0 on success and non-zero on error. Error codes:
.TP
1
General error (unknown command, invalid arguments).
.TP
2
User error (invalid input, missing argument, environment
not found).
.TP
3
System error (I/O failure, permission denied, disk full).
.TP
4
Network error (download failure, unresolved host).

All errors are structured and include a description of what went wrong
and how to fix it. Use
.B \-\-debug
to get a full stack trace.

